  "aboutMenstrual": "Your period marks the start of a new cycle. The lining of the uterus is shed, and it is normal to feel lower on energy for a few days.",
  "aboutFollicular": "After your period, estrogen rises and your body prepares to release an egg. Most people feel more energetic and focused in this phase.",
  "aboutOvulation": "An egg is released around now. This is the most fertile part of your cycle.",
  "aboutLuteal": "After ovulation, progesterone rises. In the days before your period you may notice PMS symptoms such as bloating, tender breasts or mood changes.",
  "aboutNoData": "Log your period to see which phase of your cycle you are in and get tips that fit it.",
  "tipMenstrual1": "Rest when you need to. Gentle movement such as walking or stretching can ease cramps.",
  "tipMenstrual2": "A warm compress on your lower belly may help with pain.",
  "tipMenstrual3": "Iron-rich foods like lentils, spinach and red meat, plus enough water, help you recover.",
  "tipFollicular1": "Energy usually rises in this phase, so it is a good time for more intense exercise.",
  "tipFollicular2": "A good time to start new plans or tasks that need focus.",
  "tipFollicular3": "Keep your meals balanced with protein and fresh vegetables.",
  "tipOvulation1": "This is the most fertile time of your cycle.",
  "tipOvulation2": "Mild one-sided lower belly pain or clearer discharge is usually normal around ovulation.",
  "tipOvulation3": "Energy and mood are often at their best, so enjoy being active.",
  "tipLuteal1": "PMS symptoms such as bloating or mood changes may show up. Go easy on yourself.",
  "tipLuteal2": "Cutting back on salt, caffeine and sugar can ease bloating and irritability.",
  "tipLuteal3": "Prioritise sleep and lighter exercise such as yoga or walking.",
  "tipGeneral1": "Log your period regularly. Predictions get more accurate after a few cycles.",
  "tipGeneral2": "Drink enough water and keep a steady sleep schedule.",
  "tipGeneral3": "See a doctor if your cycles are often shorter than 21 or longer than 35 days, or if the pain is severe.",
